Q3.
If the broker that is the leader for a partition fails, Kafka's controller — which monitors the cluster — detects the failure within a few seconds. It automatically promotes one of the in-sync replicas to become the new leader. Producers and consumers automatically reconnect to the new leader. The whole failover usually takes 5 to 30 seconds.
Messages are not lost because the replica already has all the messages that were committed before the failure. The replication factor is exactly how you protect against broker failures it's not just a performance feature, it's your durability guarantee.
If replication factor is 1 — no replicas — and that broker fails, the partition is unavailable and messages may be lost permanently until the broker recovers. That's why production Kafka clusters always use replication factor 2 or 3.

