Q2.
When the fourth consumer joins, Kafka triggers a rebalance — all consumers in the group briefly pause, Kafka reassigns partitions, and then they resume.
After the rebalance, there are 3 partitions and 4 consumers. Kafka assigns one partition per consumer — which means 3 consumers each get a partition and the fourth consumer gets nothing. It just sits there, connected but idle, processing zero messages.
This is a common mistake teams make when trying to scale up — they add more consumer instances thinking it'll increase throughput, but if they've already matched or exceeded the partition count, the extra consumers do nothing. The right fix is to increase the number of partitions first, then add consumers.
